(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-infective-endocarditis-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Infective Endocarditis Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with recurrent urinary tract infections was treated for pyelonephritis a month ago. She now has low-grade fevers, fatigue and a new murmur, and blood cultures grow gram-positive cocci. Which organism is most likely?","choices":["Viridans streptococci","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Streptococcus gallolyticus","Enterococci"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The genitourinary tract is the source of enterococci, which cause subacute and native valve endocarditis; urinary tract infection gives them a route into the blood. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with suspected endocarditis is too unstable to undergo a transesophageal study today. A transthoracic echocardiogram is available now. Which approach is most appropriate?","choices":["Obtain the transthoracic study now","Skip imaging entirely","Wait two weeks to image","Order a chest radiograph instead"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Transesophageal echocardiography is preferred for its sensitivity, but a transthoracic study can still show a large vegetation and should not be withheld while waiting. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with viridans streptococcal endocarditis had positive blood cultures on days 1 and 2 and her first negative culture on day 4. She asks how long her antibiotics will last. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["Until her fever settles","4-6 weeks from the day 4 culture","4-6 weeks from admission day","10 days from the first dose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Therapy is typically 4-6 weeks counted from the first negative culture, here day 4, because all bacteria in the vegetation must be killed. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has 3 weeks of fever and night sweats. Two separate blood cultures grow Streptococcus gallolyticus, and a transesophageal echocardiogram shows an oscillating mass on the aortic valve. He has no skin, eye or kidney findings. How is his case classified under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Possible endocarditis","Rejected endocarditis","Unclassifiable for now","Definite endocarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Two major criteria are met: a typical organism (Streptococcus gallolyticus) in two separate cultures and an oscillating mass on echocardiogram. Two major criteria alone establish definite endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man on chemotherapy has Candida endocarditis confirmed on blood cultures and echocardiography. Which referral is most appropriate in addition to infectious disease?","choices":["Dermatology","Cardiothoracic surgery","Addiction medicine","Physical therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fungal infection is difficult to cure with medications alone and is an indication for surgery, so cardiothoracic surgery evaluates him. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with aortic valve endocarditis has a transesophageal echocardiogram that shows an abscess around the valve ring. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Oral step-down now","Repeat echo in 6 months","Surgical evaluation","Shorten antibiotics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Abscess is an indication for surgery; antibiotics penetrate abscesses poorly, so cardiothoracic surgery evaluates her. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with suspected endocarditis has a urinalysis sent as part of her workup. Which urinalysis result is most consistent with endocarditis?","choices":["Glucose and ketones only","Bilirubin and urobilinogen","Nitrites alone, no cells","Blood, protein and white cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Urinalysis in endocarditis can show hematuria, proteinuria and pyuria, reflecting renal involvement such as glomerulonephritis or emboli. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man is on day 6 of antibiotics for aortic valve endocarditis. He still has fevers, and today's electrocardiogram shows a new conduction block not present on admission. Which complication best explains the new conduction abnormality?","choices":["Chordae tendineae rupture","Septal perivalvular abscess","Septic emboli to the lungs","Spread into the epicardium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When infection extends beyond the leaflets into the myocardium as a perivalvular abscess and reaches the upper ventricular septum, it interrupts the conduction system. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with fever and a soft murmur was started on antibiotics for suspected endocarditis. After 3 days her fever is gone, her symptoms have resolved and do not recur, and an alternative source is found. How is the suspected endocarditis classified under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Rejected","Possible","Definite","Recurrent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Endocarditis is rejected when an alternative diagnosis is established, or when symptoms resolve and do not recur with 4 days or less of antibiotic therapy. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man who injects drugs is being treated for tricuspid valve endocarditis. On day 2 he develops a new cough and sharp chest pain on inspiration, and imaging shows new nodular infiltrates in both lungs. Which complication best explains these findings?","choices":["Left heart failure","Aspiration of vomit","Septic pulmonary emboli","Drug-induced lung injury"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid vegetations throw septic emboli into the pulmonary circulation, causing cough and pleuritic chest pain, with nodular infiltrates on imaging; empyema can follow. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man with a prosthetic aortic valve has endocarditis, and his blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus. His son asks how serious this organism is. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["It is the most lethal organism","It carries a good prognosis","It rarely needs any surgery","It resolves within a week"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus is the most lethal organism in endocarditis, with mortality highest in prosthetic valve endocarditis (about 50%). — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man who injects heroin has 4 days of fever to 39.6 °C (103.3 °F), rigors, a cough and sharp chest pain that worsens with deep breaths. A holosystolic murmur is heard at the left lower sternal border, and a chest radiograph shows several small nodular infiltrates in both lungs. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute viral pericarditis","Tricuspid valve endocarditis","Community-acquired pneumonia","Mitral valve endocarditis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Injected bacteria enter the venous circulation and reach the right heart first, so the tricuspid valve is infected; its vegetation throws septic emboli to the lungs, causing cough, pleuritic pain and nodular infiltrates. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with a prosthetic mitral valve is booked for a diagnostic bronchoscopy. No biopsy or mucosal incision is planned. Which advice about endocarditis prophylaxis is most accurate?","choices":["None is needed for this procedure","Required before every bronchoscopy","A week of oral amoxicillin","Intravenous vancomycin first"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bronchoscopy does not require prophylaxis unless it involves incision of the respiratory mucosa, such as a biopsy. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has viridans streptococcal endocarditis diagnosed early, before any heart failure or abscess, and she starts appropriate antibiotics the same day. Which statement about her outlook is most accurate?","choices":["Valve replacement in most","Death within weeks is likely","Relatively good prognosis","Highest mortality of any"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Viridans streptococcal endocarditis has a relatively good prognosis if antibiotic treatment starts before complications occur. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman being evaluated for endocarditis had a soft systolic murmur documented for 10 years. Today the murmur sounds a little louder. How does this change count under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["It is an immunologic phenomenon","It confirms definite endocarditis","It does not meet the major criterion","It meets the major criterion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. New valvular regurgitation counts as endocardial involvement, but an increase or change in a preexisting murmur is not sufficient. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has 6 weeks of aching and stiffness in her shoulders and hips, fatigue and a low-grade fever. Her erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein are high, but no murmur is heard and three sets of blood cultures are negative. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Subacute endocarditis","Atrial myxoma","Antiphospholipid syndrome","Polymyalgia rheumatica"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Polymyalgia rheumatica sits in the differential of endocarditis because it causes myalgia with elevated inflammatory markers, but there is no murmur and cultures are negative. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 9-year-old with unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart disease is scheduled for a tonsillectomy. Which is the most appropriate step regarding endocarditis?","choices":["Give antibiotics only afterward","Cancel all future surgery","Give prophylaxis before surgery","No prophylaxis is needed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart disease is a high-risk pediatric condition, and tonsillectomy is an invasive respiratory procedure with mucosal incision, so prophylaxis is given beforehand. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 2-month-old infant with a central line has poor feeding, fast breathing, tachycardia and a changing murmur. Blood cultures are positive and echocardiography shows a vegetation. Which complications are particularly common in infants because of septic emboli?","choices":["Glomerulonephritis only","Osteomyelitis, meningitis or pneumonia","Janeway lesions and Roth spots","Heart failure from valve stenosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Septic emboli are common in neonates and infants, producing osteomyelitis, meningitis or pneumonia, and neurologic signs such as seizures, hemiparesis and apnea. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"An 83-year-old woman is ready for discharge on day 9 of a planned 6-week course of intravenous antibiotics for endocarditis. She is deconditioned and cannot yet walk safely on her own. Which part of the multidisciplinary plan addresses this?","choices":["Stopping the antibiotics","Rehabilitation placement","Addiction medicine","Immediate valve surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The multidisciplinary team plans disposition, including rehabilitation, while infectious disease continues antibiotic therapy and outpatient monitoring. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman on long-term immunosuppression has endocarditis, and her blood cultures grow Candida. Which complication is most likely?","choices":["Embolic large artery occlusion","Rapid cure with medication","Isolated rheumatic carditis","Pericardial knock"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In fungal endocarditis the most likely complication is embolism and occlusion of larger peripheral arteries from embolization. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with a native mitral valve has 5 weeks of low-grade fever, fatigue and a new murmur. Three sets of blood cultures have just been drawn, and the organism is not yet known. Which empiric regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone","Oral amoxicillin, single dose","Nafcillin plus rifampin","Oral doxycycline for 2 weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Empiric therapy covers staphylococci, streptococci and enterococci; vancomycin plus ceftriaxone is the regimen for a subacute presentation, native valve endocarditis, or a prosthetic valve in place more than a year. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with a prosthetic aortic valve is having a tooth extracted tomorrow. He has no drug allergies and takes pills without difficulty. Which prophylaxis is most appropriate?","choices":["No prophylaxis is needed","Oral amoxicillin, 30-60 minutes before","Oral amoxicillin for 7 days after","Clindamycin before the visit"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A high-risk patient (prosthetic valve) having an extraction gets a single dose of oral amoxicillin 30-60 minutes before the procedure, targeting viridans streptococci. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man had his pacemaker generator changed 3 weeks ago. He now has a fever of 38.8 °C (101.8 °F), and imaging shows a vegetation on a pacemaker lead. Which organisms cause the largest share of this type of endocarditis?","choices":["Coagulase-negative staphylococci","Viridans streptococci","Gram-negative coccobacilli","Enterococci"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Device endocarditis is caused mostly by coagulase-negative staphylococci (about 41%) and Staphylococcus aureus (about 36%), and it can appear within weeks of implantation or a generator change. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man who injects drugs has finished a course of intravenous antibiotics for tricuspid valve endocarditis and is preparing for discharge. He says he wants help to stay safe. Which consultation should be arranged?","choices":["Dermatology","Ophthalmology","Nephrology","Addiction medicine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Addiction medicine is consulted for harm reduction in people who inject drugs, part of the multidisciplinary care with infectious disease and cardiothoracic surgery. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman had a mechanical mitral valve placed 4 years ago. She has 5 weeks of low-grade fever and fatigue, and blood cultures are pending. Which organism is most likely in her prosthetic valve infection?","choices":["Escherichia coli","Viridans streptococci","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Candida albicans"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Late prosthetic valve endocarditis is caused by viridans streptococci, while early infection (around surgery) is caused by coagulase-negative staphylococci and Staphylococcus aureus. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with fever has four sets of blood cultures drawn. One set grows coagulase-negative staphylococci, and the other three are negative. How should this single positive culture be counted under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["As the major blood culture criterion","As a vascular phenomenon","As definite proof of endocarditis","Not counted, a likely contaminant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Single positive cultures for coagulase-negative staphylococci (and diphtheroids) are excluded from the microbiologic criterion because they are common culture contaminants. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with previous infective endocarditis brings an old wallet card that lists clindamycin for her dental prophylaxis. She has a penicillin allergy. Which counseling point is most accurate?","choices":["Keep using clindamycin","Switch to amoxicillin","She no longer needs any","Clindamycin is dropped"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is no longer recommended for prophylaxis because of the risk of Clostridioides difficile infection; she should be given an alternative such as azithromycin. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has 3 days of fever to 40.0 °C (104.0 °F), rigors and a new murmur, and he looks toxic. Which organism is a classic cause of this tempo of infective endocarditis?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Viridans streptococci","Coagulase-negative staphylococci","Gram-negative coccobacilli"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An abrupt, toxic, high-fever course is acute endocarditis, caused by virulent organisms such as Staphylococcus aureus and beta-hemolytic streptococci. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic kidney disease has native valve endocarditis from penicillin-susceptible viridans streptococci. The team discusses a 2-week regimen that adds gentamicin. Which choice should be avoided?","choices":["Ceftriaxone for the full course","Monitoring renal function","The 2-week gentamicin regimen","Penicillin G for the full course"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The 2-week regimen with gentamicin is reserved for uncomplicated endocarditis with a rapid response and no chronic kidney disease; gentamicin is nephrotoxic, so he needs a standard course without it. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with acute endocarditis asks why the team is so worried when she felt well a week ago. Which statement about untreated acute endocarditis is accurate?","choices":["It smolders harmlessly for years","It only threatens the lungs","It can be fatal within weeks","It resolves without treatment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute endocarditis rapidly damages cardiac structures and seeds extracardiac sites; if untreated, it progresses to death within weeks. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 10"}]};
